{{ template "chart.header" . }}
{{ template "chart.description" . }}

This directory is a complete layout following [SPEC.md](../SPEC.md): a
catalog, two clusters and three environments, plus the small Helm chart that
turns it into Argo CD Applications. This page walks through how the pieces fit,
assuming you know Kubernetes and Helm but little of Argo CD.

## Words used on this page

- An **environment** (`envs/<env>/`) is a set of applications deployed
  together: `platform`, `staging`, `prod`. It runs on one or more clusters.
- A **cluster** (`clusters/<cluster>/`) is a Kubernetes cluster: `lab-1`, and
  `airgap-1`, which has no Internet access.
- A **placement** is an environment on one of its clusters, such as `prod` on
  `airgap-1`. It is what actually gets deployed.
- An **app** is something installable: Grafana, cert-manager. The
  **catalog** (`catalog/apps/`) holds the apps any environment can install; a
  cluster can also define apps of its own (`clusters/<cluster>/apps/`).
- An **instance** is an app installed in an environment, under a name. Most
  instances are named after their app; `ceph-hdd` and `ceph-ssd` are two
  instances of the app `rook-ceph-cluster`.
- A **source** is one thing an app deploys, usually a Helm chart, named in the
  app's `app.yaml`.
- A **layer** is one level of configuration of an instance: the app's
  default, then the environment's, the cluster's and the placement's changes.
- A **property** is a named value, such as a registry address, set for the
  whole catalog, an environment or a cluster, and injected into the apps that
  ask for it.

## Argo CD in a nutshell

Argo CD runs in a Kubernetes cluster and keeps other clusters (and its own) in
line with what a Git repository describes. Three of its objects matter here:

- An **Application** says *what* to deploy and *where*: one or more sources (a
  Helm chart, a directory of manifests in Git) and a destination (a cluster and
  a namespace). Argo CD renders the sources, compares the result with the
  cluster, and applies it when you **sync** the Application. A source can also
  just lend its files to the others: the Applications here read their Helm
  values files from this repository that way.
- An Application can deploy anything, including **other Applications**. An
  Application whose sources render Application manifests is often called an
  *app of apps*: syncing it creates or updates the Applications it describes.
- An **ApplicationSet** generates Applications from a template, here one per
  cluster registered in Argo CD.

Nothing in this example deploys on its own: every Application shows what would
change, and applies it when someone syncs it. The one automatic step is the
ApplicationSet's: it creates, updates and deletes the `layout-<cluster>`
Applications themselves, one per registered cluster.

## Anatomy of an app directory

Every layer of every instance is a directory of the same shape. Here is the
default layer of Grafana, in the catalog:

```
catalog/apps/grafana/
├── app.yaml                     what the app deploys: its sources, their
│                                versions, and which properties go where
├── values/
│   └── chart.yaml               Helm values of the source named `chart`
│                                in app.yaml (the file is named after it)
└── manifests/                   extra Kubernetes objects, as a Kustomize
    │                            Component (a reusable piece of Kustomize)
    ├── kustomization.yaml       lists every file below
    └── resources/
        └── external-secrets.io/          the object's API group
            └── ExternalSecret/           its kind
                └── grafana-admin.yaml    its name
```

Files under `manifests/` are named after the object they hold — its
*identity*, `<group>/<kind>/<name>`, which error messages quote — so the path
is enough to find one:

- objects of the core API (`apiVersion: v1`: ConfigMap, Secret…) use `core`
  as their group, as in
  [core/ConfigMap/grafana-airgap.yaml](envs/prod/clusters/airgap-1/instances/grafana/manifests/resources/core/ConfigMap/grafana-airgap.yaml);
- an object that sets its own namespace gets it after an underscore, as the
  ApplicationSet
  [ApplicationSet/layout_argocd.yaml](catalog/apps/layout/manifests/resources/argoproj.io/ApplicationSet/layout_argocd.yaml),
  which lives in `argocd`;
- `resources/` adds objects, and `patches/` changes the objects of the layers
  below. A patch is a *strategic merge patch*: a partial copy of the object
  holding only the fields to change, with the object's `apiVersion`, `kind`
  and `name`. A patch holding `$patch: delete` removes the object, as
  [the letsencrypt issuer on airgap-1](clusters/airgap-1/overrides/cert-manager/manifests/patches/cert-manager.io/ClusterIssuer/letsencrypt.yaml),
  which cannot reach Let's Encrypt.

Each layer of an instance has its own directory, and any of them may be
missing:

| Layer | Directory | Applies to |
| --- | --- | --- |
| default | `catalog/apps/<app>/`, or `clusters/<cluster>/apps/<app>/` for a cluster's own app | every instance of the app |
| environment | `envs/<env>/instances/<instance>/` | the instance, on every cluster of its environment |
| cluster | `clusters/<cluster>/overrides/<app>/` | every instance of a catalog app on that cluster |
| placement | `envs/<env>/clusters/<cluster>/instances/<instance>/` | the instance, on that cluster only |

Higher layers win: the cluster overrides the environment, and the placement
overrides everything. Each environment lists its instances in its `env.yaml`,
for instance [envs/prod/env.yaml](envs/prod/env.yaml):

- an instance with no field is the app of the same name;
- `app: <app>` installs an app under another name, which is how one app is
  installed twice;
- `local: true` marks an app that exists only in this environment: its
  definition is its `instances/<instance>/` directory, which a placement layer
  can still adjust per cluster, like `whoami` in
  [envs/staging/env.yaml](envs/staging/env.yaml);
- `disabled:` under a cluster skips instances on that cluster.

## Every field of app.yaml

An `app.yaml` holds only what its layer changes: the catalog's states the
whole app, a higher layer one field or two. All the fields, on an imaginary
app:

```yaml
namespace: monitoring          # where the instance is deployed; without it,
                               # <env>-<instance>
sources:                       # what the app deploys, by name
  chart:                       # a source named "chart": its Helm values are
                               # in values/chart.yaml
    type: helm                 # helm, kustomize, jsonnet or directory
    repoURL:                   # where it comes from: a literal URL, or a
      property: ghcrCharts     # property holding one
    repository: grafana/helm-charts  # appended to repoURL after a "/"
    chart: grafana             # the chart; a Git source has `path` instead
    targetRevision: "10.5.15"  # always quoted: YAML reads 1.10 as 1.1
    releaseName: grafana       # Helm release name; without it,
                               # <env>-<instance>
    properties:                # Helm parameter <- property name
      global.imageRegistry: dockerImages
manifests:
  properties:                  # object in manifests/ <- field <- property
    external-secrets.io/ExternalSecret/grafana-admin:
      /spec/secretStoreRef/name: secretStore
application:                   # settings of the Argo CD Application
  syncOptions:
    - ServerSideApply=true
  ignoreDifferences: []
```

Five things to know about them:

- **A property wins over the values files.** `global.imageRegistry` above is
  set from `dockerImages` after every values file is read, so writing it in a
  `values/chart.yaml` changes nothing. To set it by hand in one layer, remove
  the mapping there first: `global.imageRegistry: null` under the source's
  `properties`.
- **A property must exist for every placement of the instance.** One defined
  only in `clusters/airgap-1/properties.yaml` fails the render of `lab-1`:
  give it a default in [catalog/properties.yaml](catalog/properties.yaml).
- **`null` removes.** In any layer, `<field>: null` deletes what the layers
  below set, a whole source included.
- **Large CRDs need `ServerSideApply=true`.** A client-side apply stores the
  whole object in an annotation capped at 256 KiB; external-secrets and Rook
  ship CRDs above it, hence their `application.syncOptions`. When
  `ignoreDifferences` is set, the wiring adds `RespectIgnoreDifferences=true`,
  so that a sync leaves those fields alone instead of rewriting them.
- **Maps merge, lists replace.** A layer that writes `application.syncOptions`
  or `ignoreDifferences` replaces the whole list of the layers below: it
  repeats the entries it keeps. The wiring's own sync options, such as
  `CreateNamespace=true`, are kept whatever the list, unless it names them
  with another value.

Under `manifests.properties`, a field is a *JSON pointer*: the path to it in
the object, one `/` per level, as in `/spec/secretStoreRef/name`.

## Properties

A property is set in up to four files. For an instance, the most specific one
that sets it wins:

| File | Applies to |
| --- | --- |
| `envs/<env>/clusters/<cluster>/properties.yaml` | one environment on one cluster |
| `clusters/<cluster>/properties.yaml` | every environment on that cluster |
| `envs/<env>/properties.yaml` | one environment, on every cluster |
| `catalog/properties.yaml` | everything: the defaults |

The cluster wins over the environment because what a cluster can reach — a
registry, a Vault — is a fact about the cluster. A property only reaches the
apps whose `app.yaml` maps it: changing one changes nothing for an app that
does not ask for it.

Some objects exist once per cluster and serve every environment on it, such
as the `ClusterSecretStore` external-secrets uses. One environment that needs
a different variant does not change it: it adds a second object next to it,
in the cluster layer of the app that owns it
(`clusters/<cluster>/overrides/<app>/manifests/`), and points its own apps at
it with a property set for that environment on that cluster.

## How the pieces fit

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    appset["ApplicationSet layout<br/>catalog/apps/layout/"]
    parent["Application layout-&lt;cluster&gt;<br/>one per registered cluster"]
    chart["this Helm chart<br/>templates/"]
    files["catalog/, clusters/, envs/"]
    child["Application &lt;env&gt;.&lt;instance&gt;.&lt;cluster&gt;<br/>one per instance"]
    workloads["workloads on &lt;cluster&gt;<br/>cert-manager, grafana, …"]

    appset -->|"generates, per cluster"| parent
    parent -->|"renders"| chart
    chart -->|"reads"| files
    chart -->|"emits"| child
    child -->|"renders the app's chart,<br/>values and manifests"| workloads
    child -.->|"platform.layout.lab-1 deploys"| appset
```

1. The **ApplicationSet `layout`** creates one Application `layout-<cluster>`
   per cluster Argo CD knows. It is itself an app of the catalog,
   [catalog/apps/layout/](catalog/apps/layout/), installed by the `platform`
   environment on `lab-1`, the cluster running Argo CD: the layout deploys its
   own wiring (the dotted arrow).
2. Each **`layout-<cluster>`** renders this chart for its cluster. The chart
   reads `envs/*/env.yaml`, keeps the environments placed on that cluster, and
   for each of their instances merges the layers.
3. The chart emits one **Application per instance**, named
   `<env>.<instance>.<cluster>`. Syncing it deploys the instance into its
   namespace — the one its `app.yaml` names, `<env>-<instance>` otherwise.

The environments split the work. `platform` holds what a cluster needs once,
whoever uses it: cert-manager, external-secrets and the Rook operator, which
install the CRDs the other apps rely on, the Traefik ingress controller, and
the layout's own ApplicationSet. `staging` and `prod` hold the applications,
and can both run Grafana on `lab-1` because each instance gets its own
namespace and Helm release name.

[templates/](templates/) is the whole chart. When it finds a mistake, the
render of `layout-<cluster>` fails with a message naming the file and the rule
of [SPEC.md](../SPEC.md), so the error shows in Argo CD before anything is
applied. It checks the catalog, the environments placed on that cluster and
the cluster's own directory — so a typo in `staging` never blocks `airgap-1`,
which does not run it. It checks names, files that match nothing, how each
instance resolves, the sources, the values files, every `manifests/`
directory, the properties, and that no two instances share a Helm release or
deploy the same object of `manifests/` in one namespace. What it cannot see surfaces when the instance's own Application
renders or syncs: the objects a chart renders, a property mapped to a field
that does not exist, and two apps installing the same cluster-wide object —
which is why such apps go in `platform`, once per cluster.

## Following one instance

Take Grafana in the `prod` environment on the air-gapped cluster `airgap-1`.

**It is declared.** [envs/prod/env.yaml](envs/prod/env.yaml) places `prod` on
`lab-1` and `airgap-1` and lists `grafana` among its instances, with no
field: it is the catalog app of the same name.

**Its layers are found:**

| Layer | Directory | What it adds here |
| --- | --- | --- |
| default | [catalog/apps/grafana/](catalog/apps/grafana/) | the chart, default values, an `ExternalSecret` for the admin password |
| environment | [envs/prod/instances/grafana/](envs/prod/instances/grafana/) | values for every `prod` cluster |
| cluster | `clusters/airgap-1/overrides/grafana/` | nothing: the directory does not exist |
| placement | [envs/prod/clusters/airgap-1/instances/grafana/](envs/prod/clusters/airgap-1/instances/grafana/) | a `ConfigMap` and values for `prod` on `airgap-1` only |

**Its properties are resolved**, in the same order: the catalog's
[defaults](catalog/properties.yaml), then [`prod`'s](envs/prod/properties.yaml),
then [`airgap-1`'s](clusters/airgap-1/properties.yaml). `prod` points container
images at the company's Docker Hub cache, but `airgap-1` cannot reach it and points them at
its Harbor registry; the cluster is more specific than the environment, so
Harbor wins. The app's [app.yaml](catalog/apps/grafana/app.yaml) says where
each property lands: the chart's registry, a Helm parameter, a field of the
`ExternalSecret`.

**The Application comes out** as `prod.grafana.airgap-1` in
[rendered/airgap-1.yaml](rendered/airgap-1.yaml):

- the Grafana chart, read from the Harbor mirror. Charts are downloaded by
  Argo CD on `lab-1`, not by `airgap-1`: the chart registry property matters
  when the central Argo CD must use the mirror too, or when an air-gapped
  cluster runs its own Argo CD;
- container images pulled from Harbor too. `airgap-1`'s nodes are configured
  to pull every public registry through Harbor, so an image no property
  rewrites still arrives; Grafana's and cert-manager's are rewritten
  explicitly, which is how a cluster without such node mirrors would do it;
- the values files of every layer, in order, read from this repository. They
  appear as `$layout/…`: `$layout` names the Application's source that lends
  this repository's files, the one carrying `ref: layout`;
- `global.imageRegistry` set to Harbor, and the `ExternalSecret` reading from
  the cluster's secret store;
- the manifests of every layer stacked on [base/](base/), an empty Kustomize
  base.

## Bootstrapping

You need Argo CD 3.0 or later running on `lab-1`, the `argocd` command logged
in to it (`argocd login`), and `kubectl` pointed at `lab-1`. Argo CD on
`lab-1` must reach the API server of `airgap-1` and the mirrors `airgap-1`
uses: it renders and applies everything from there.

Charts whose `repoURL` has no `https://` come from OCI registries, the
container-image protocol that registries also use for Helm charts. Tell
Argo CD so with a credential template per registry, which covers every chart
path under it (and the same for the Harbor paths `airgap-1` uses):

```sh
argocd repocreds add quay.io --type helm --enable-oci
```

```sh
argocd repocreds add ghcr.io --type helm --enable-oci
```

Register every cluster under its name in the layout, including the one
running Argo CD, so that the ApplicationSet sees it:

```sh
argocd cluster add <lab-1-context> --name lab-1 --in-cluster
```

```sh
argocd cluster add <airgap-1-context> --name airgap-1
```

From this directory, render the Applications of `lab-1` once by hand, and sync
the one that deploys the ApplicationSet (the Sync button in the Argo CD UI
does the same as `argocd app sync`):

```sh
helm template layout . --set cluster=lab-1 --set layout.repoURL=https://github.com/therealm-tech/argocd-layout-spec --set layout.targetRevision=HEAD --set layout.path=examples | kubectl apply -n argocd -f -
```

```sh
argocd app sync platform.layout.lab-1
```

The ApplicationSet now exists and creates `layout-lab-1` and
`layout-airgap-1`. Sync both: `layout-lab-1` becomes the owner of the
Applications applied by hand — Argo CD then updates and deletes them with it —
and `layout-airgap-1` creates those of `airgap-1`:

```sh
argocd app sync layout-lab-1 layout-airgap-1
```

Then sync the instances, those of `platform` first, since the others need the
CRDs they install. `argocd app list` shows them all; the UI does the same in
a few clicks:

```sh
argocd app sync platform.cert-manager.lab-1 platform.external-secrets.lab-1 platform.rook-ceph.lab-1 platform.traefik.lab-1
```

## Everyday changes

Every change is a commit; Argo CD notices it within a few minutes, or at once
with a webhook or the Refresh button. What to sync then depends on what
changed:

- **The content of a values file or of a manifest** that already exists: only
  the Applications of the instances using it turn OutOfSync. Sync them.
- **Anything else** — an `env.yaml`, an `app.yaml`, a properties file, or a
  values file or `manifests/` directory added or removed — changes the
  Applications themselves: sync `layout-<cluster>` first, then the instances
  it shows as changed.

The recipes, with the file each one touches:

| To | Edit | Rule that applies |
| --- | --- | --- |
| install an app in an environment | `instances:` in `envs/<env>/env.yaml` | an app that installs CRDs or other cluster-wide objects goes in `platform`, once per cluster |
| install an app twice | two entries with the same `app:` in `env.yaml` | whatever the chart names without its release name, such as Rook's StorageClasses, must differ: see `ceph-hdd` and `ceph-ssd` |
| skip an instance on one cluster | `disabled:` under that cluster in `env.yaml` | |
| change a Helm value | `values/<source>.yaml` in the layer that matches the scope: default, environment, cluster or placement | maps merge, lists replace; a mapped property wins over the file |
| change a chart version | `targetRevision` in the `app.yaml` of the matching layer | quoted |
| change what a cluster can reach (a registry, a Vault) | `clusters/<cluster>/properties.yaml` | every property used must keep a value for every placement |
| add, change or remove a Kubernetes object | `manifests/` of the matching layer: `resources/` to add, `patches/` to change or delete | the file is named after the object; a patch on a custom resource replaces its lists whole |
| add an Argo CD sync option or ignored difference | `application:` in the matching `app.yaml` | lists replace: repeat the entries of the layers below; the wiring's options stay |
| deploy another chart with an instance, in its namespace | a new entry under `sources:` in the matching `app.yaml`, its values in `values/<source>.yaml` | give it a `releaseName`: two charts of one instance cannot share a release name |
| use another variant of a cluster-wide object for one environment | a second object in `clusters/<cluster>/overrides/<app>/manifests/`, and a property in `envs/<env>/clusters/<cluster>/properties.yaml` | see "Properties" above |
| share a local app with another environment | move `envs/<env>/instances/<instance>/` to `catalog/apps/<app>/`, and drop `local: true` | |

When a value does not show up, read the instance's Application in Argo CD
(the App details, or `argocd app get <env>.<instance>.<cluster> -o yaml`): its
`valueFiles` are applied in order, the last one winning, and its `parameters`
come after all of them. The file or the property that sets the value is
there.

A synced Application whose objects differ from Git again is **OutOfSync**:
that is what a sync clears.

Three changes need more care:

- **Add a cluster**: register it in Argo CD, create `clusters/<cluster>/` if
  it needs anything specific, and list it under `clusters:` in the
  environments placed on it. Disable there the instances it cannot run: the
  apps of another cluster, like `traefik`, and the layout's own `layout`,
  which belongs to the cluster running Argo CD only.
- **Rename an instance**: its Application, and its namespace and release
  names unless its `app.yaml` sets them, change with it — which for a
  database or a Ceph cluster means a second, empty one next to the old. Pin
  them first in the environment layer (`namespace: prod-ceph-ssd`, and
  `releaseName: prod-ceph-ssd` under the source), then rename.
- **Remove an instance**: delete its workloads first, with
  `argocd app delete <env>.<instance>.<cluster> --cascade`, then remove it
  from `env.yaml`. The other way round, syncing `layout-<cluster>` with
  pruning — deleting what is no longer in Git — removes only its Application,
  and leaves its workloads running.

[examples-confidential/](../examples-confidential/) is a second layout, with
its own environments and clusters, that uses this catalog from another
repository — for clusters whose configuration must stay private.
[catalog/Chart.yaml](catalog/Chart.yaml) is what lets it: it packages the
catalog as a Helm chart.

## Chart values

The ApplicationSet sets `cluster` and `layout` for each `layout-<cluster>`;
`project` and `argocdNamespace` keep their defaults. You only set values
yourself when bootstrapping.

